@node pop-server
@section pop-server

pop-server is a web-server that helps you simulate behaviours and test integrations with external systems.

pop-server is a lightweight HTTP server that allows dynamic behavior registration and retrieval. It acts as a simple puppet server where you can define request-response behaviors dynamically via HTTP requests. This can be useful for testing, mocking APIs, or creating programmable server responses.

Find the project here: @uref{https://codeberg.org/jjba23/pop-server}

You can use @code{pop_server} in pop-tests easily:

@code{enabled} (optional, boolean) - Whether to enable pop-server in a container or not

@code{image_name} (optional, string) - Container image for pop-server, by default @code{jjba23/pop-server}

@code{image_tag} (optional, string) - Container image tag for pop-server, by default @code{0.3.0}


You can then  have it prepared with certain behaviors (under @code{behaviors} ):

@code{path} (required, string) - what URL path should be called to trigger behavior

@code{method} (required, string) - what HTTP method should be used to trigger behavior

@code{status} (required, string) - what HTTP status should be returned

@code{content_type} (required, string) - what content type should be returned as HTTP header

@code{body_file} (required, string) - path to the file to load data from, that will be returned as HTTP body

@node pop keycloak
@section pop keycloak

You can use Keycloak in pop-tests easily and have it auto-setup.

@code{enabled} (optional, boolean) - Whether to enable Keycloak in a container or not

@code{image_name} (optional, string) - Container image for Keycloak, by default @code{keycloak/keycloak}

@code{image_tag} (optional, string) - Container image tag for Keycloak, by default @code{26.1}

@code{realm} (required, string) – Name of the Keycloak realm to be created.

@code{client_id} (required, string) – OAuth ID of the Keycloak client that will be created for service authentication.

@code{client_secret} (optional, string) – OAuth secret  of the Keycloak client that will be created for service authentication.

@code{email}  (optional, string) – Email of the Keycloak user that will be created for service authentication.

@code{username} (required, string) - Username of the Keycloak client that will be created for service authentication.

@code{password} (required, string) - Password  of the Keycloak client that will be created for service authentication.

@code{roles} (optional, list of strings) - Names of roles to be created realm-wide and associated to the user

@code{scopes} (optional, list of strings) - Names of scopes to be created realm-wide and associated to the OAuth client

@node Example tests using the DSL
@chapter Example tests, using the DSL

For example tests and learning demos, refer to the scenarios directory: @uref{../../resources/scenarios}

Here follows also a trivial example of pop-tests.

In this example we spin up a REST API written in Scala, with SBT, and poll its @code{/health} endpoint for 90 seconds or until healthy. Then we perform some checks on the response of certain HTTP endpoints. These checks are performed using @code{jql} and this allows us to check deeply nested attributes of the JSON response.

Reminder: the tool supports YAML, TOML and JSON, but YAML is as of now the preferred format.

@example
pop_services:
  - id: "green-energy-mix"
    dir: "$HOME/Ontwikkeling/Werk"
    base_url: "http://localhost:50000"
    start_cmd_args:
      - 'sbt "greenEnergyMix/run"'
    env_vars:
      SOME_TOKEN: "0027809ce9"
    health_check:
      path: "/health"
      patience_seconds: 120

pop_actions:
  - name: "Check that the forecast endpoint returns a 200 with a correct data, with load and solar"
    assert_url: "/api/v1/forecast"
    service_id: "green-energy-mix"
    json_body_patterns:
      - pattern: '"meta""forecastDate"'
        condition: '!= ""'
      - pattern: '"data"[0]"load"'
        condition: "> 0"
      - pattern: '"data"[0]"solar"'
        condition: ">= 0"

@end example

